\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces DISCELL in four panels. \textbf  {a},\nobreakspace  {}Spill-over: a cell (blue) receives transcripts from its neighbours in the pruned Delaunay graph, in proportion to the kernel weights $\beta _{ij}$ (arrow widths); the niche descriptor $\mathbf  {c}_i$ reads the neighbours' types and the surrounding tissue image, with the cell itself masked out. \textbf  {b},\nobreakspace  {}The generative model (\cref  {eq:gen-z,eq:gen-w,eq:gen-rho,eq:gen-rhobar,eq:gen-p,eq:gen-x}): the intrinsic state $\mathbf  {z}_i$ and the response $\mathbf  {w}_i$, whose prior is set by the niche and the type, decode to the cell's own composition $\bm  {\rho }_i$, which is mixed with the neighbours' compositions at the fixed fraction $\kappa $. An adversary keeps the niche out of $\mathbf  {z}_i$ given the type. \textbf  {c},\nobreakspace  {}Why $\kappa $ is swept (\cref  {prop:kappa-bound}): on the simplex of three genes, each assumed $\kappa $ implies a clean composition on a line running from the observed composition $\mathbf  {p}_i$ away from the neighbours' influx $\bar  {\bm  {\rho }}_i$; every $\kappa $ fits until a gene's share would turn negative at $\bar  \kappa _i$, so the data bound $\kappa $ from above only. Schematic. \textbf  {d},\nobreakspace  {}The breakdown point (\cref  {def:breakdown}): a finding is refitted at every $\kappa $ of the grid, and $\kappa ^\star $ is the smallest $\kappa $ at which its interval reaches its null value. Schematic.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{3}{DISCELL in four panels. \textbf {a},~Spill-over: a cell (blue) receives transcripts from its neighbours in the pruned Delaunay graph, in proportion to the kernel weights $\beta _{ij}$ (arrow widths); the niche descriptor $\vc _i$ reads the neighbours' types and the surrounding tissue image, with the cell itself masked out. \textbf {b},~The generative model (\cref {eq:gen-z,eq:gen-w,eq:gen-rho,eq:gen-rhobar,eq:gen-p,eq:gen-x}): the intrinsic state $\vz _i$ and the response $\vw _i$, whose prior is set by the niche and the type, decode to the cell's own composition $\vrho _i$, which is mixed with the neighbours' compositions at the fixed fraction $\kappa $. An adversary keeps the niche out of $\vz _i$ given the type. \textbf {c},~Why $\kappa $ is swept (\cref {prop:kappa-bound}): on the simplex of three genes, each assumed $\kappa $ implies a clean composition on a line running from the observed composition $\vp _i$ away from the neighbours' influx $\rhobar _i$; every $\kappa $ fits until a gene's share would turn negative at $\bar \kappa _i$, so the data bound $\kappa $ from above only. Schematic. \textbf {d},~The breakdown point (\cref {def:breakdown}): a finding is refitted at every $\kappa $ of the grid, and $\kappa ^\star $ is the smallest $\kappa $ at which its interval reaches its null value. Schematic.\relax }{figure.caption.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig:overview@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][2][]3}}
